course: dictionary
lesson: 13506
title: Words about time: imminently
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Imminently» is an adverb. It tells you when something will happen, and it means very soon, at any moment. Adverbs often tell you how, when or where, so ask: when is it going to happen?
explain: «The storm is expected imminently, so the fishing boats have all come home.» The boats came home, so the storm must be very close. The clue is in the words around the new word.
explain: Stories use this word to build tension. «Mia heard footsteps on the stairs. The thief would arrive imminently.» The reader feels the danger is near. To check your meaning, swap in «very soon» or «any moment now» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Imminently means very soon, about to happen at any moment.
fact: It is an adverb, so it tells you when something happens.
fact: News reports and official announcements often use «imminently», as in «the report is due imminently».
fact: Common phrases are: expected imminently, due imminently and arriving imminently.
exercise: «The bus is arriving imminently, so get your bag.» What does imminently mean? | *Very soon | Last week | Never | Very slowly
exercise: «The decision is due imminently.» Which word tells you when the decision will happen? | the | is | decision | *imminently
exercise: A news reporter says, «The storm will hit imminently.» What does this tell viewers? | *The storm will hit very soon | The storm has already gone | The storm is a funny story | The storm is far away and will never come
test: «The rocket will launch imminently.» What does imminently mean? | Quietly | Far in the future | Yesterday | *Very soon
test: Which swap keeps the meaning of «imminently» in «The guests are arriving imminently»? | *At any moment | Gently | Lately | Slowly
exam: Ella reads, «The alarm will sound imminently.» Which swap keeps the same meaning? | very far away | very quietly | very last year | *very soon
